  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIn previous forecasts, we mentioned the potential for a cluster of \nthunderstorms late Sunday night into Monday morning. In recent \nruns, high- resolution forecast guidance has backed off on this \nidea for our local forecast area. \n\nInstability will build Monday afternoon, particularly across \nsoutheast Ohio and northern West Virginia, with 1,000 to 2,000 J/kg \nof CAPE expected during that timeframe. Isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms may develop Monday afternoon across these areas as \nrounds of vorticity flow northwest to southeast from the Great Lakes.\n\nModels have been consistently showing the potential for a \nthunderstorm complex to impact the area Monday night into Tuesday, \nbut they are still having a difficult time agreeing on a consistent \ntrack and location.\n\nThe best potential for thunderstorms will occur in the early morning \nhours of Tuesday, ahead of a few robust vort. maxes and an\nadvancing cold front. High- resolution mesoscale models are \ncontinuing to show the potential for a bowing cluster of \nthunderstorms approaching from the Great Lakes during this time.\nThe synoptic pattern will feature a strong 250-mb jet streak \naligning northwest to southeast from Wisconsin to northern Ohio,\nplacing the middle and upper Ohio Valley region in the \nfavorable exit region for upward motion. In addition, robust \n500-mb vorticity will approach from the Great Lakes. 0-6km shear\nwill be robust at 45-50 kts, and some models still show a \nfairly high amount of instability during the predawn hours of \nTuesday (1,000 to 1,500 J/kg from northeast KY through northern \nWV). A low-level jet will advect 65 to 70 degree dew points into\nthe region, and precipitable water values will range from 2.00 \nto 2.25 inches.\n\nThe atmospheric setup early Tuesday morning does not look favorable \nfor catastrophic flooding like what was seen last week because \nstorms should be moving at a fairly quick pace. However, localized \nflash flooding will certainly be a concern, especially in areas that \nwere recently hit hard with flooding last week.\n\nDepending on how quickly storms exit Tuesday morning, there may be \nan opportunity for the atmosphere to destabilize again Tuesday \nafternoon before the main cold front approaches between 18Z Tuesday \nand 00Z Wednesday. This is highly uncertain at this time, but \nanother round of thunderstorms Tuesday afternoon is not out of the \nquestion.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWednesday through Friday should be fairly quiet as ridging brings \ndry weather. However, chances of showers and thunderstorms will \nreturn next weekend as another mid-level disturbance approaches. \nTemperatures will range from the middle to upper 70s in the \nmountains and the middle to upper 80s in the lowlands from Wednesday \nthrough next weekend.",
